#[doc("""
What a run does with a message that is not its kalku's.

A run waits on a mailbox, and kaikai's `receive` takes whatever is in it:
there is no selective receive to pick out the replies a run is waiting
for. Inside a server that mailbox belongs to a session, and a client can
ask a question — what is happening, stop — while the run is still going.
Dropping those messages is what makes a client wait for an answer that
will never come.

An effect rather than a branch, for the same reason `Telling` is one: the
orchestrator has no business knowing that a server exists. A run says
*this one is not mine*; a server answers it from the state it holds, and
a command line drops it, because on a command line nothing else is
talking into that mailbox.
""")]

import kalku.orchestrator.kalku_proc

#[doc("A message that arrived mid-run and belongs to whoever is hosting the run.")]
pub effect Attending {
  not_mine(m: OrchMsg) : Unit
}

#[doc("""
Run with nothing else listening, which is what a command line is.

The message is dropped, deliberately: the only sender is a kalku this run
has stopped caring about — one it killed, whose reader says so once —
and there is nobody else to hand it to.
""")]
pub fn alone[a, e](body: () -> a / Attending + e) : a / e = handle {
  body()
} with Attending {
  not_mine(_m, resume) -> resume(())
  return(x) -> x
}

#[doc("Run with every stray message handed to `hear`, which is what a server does.")]
pub fn attended[a, e](hear: (OrchMsg) -> Unit / e, body: () -> a / Attending + e) : a / e = handle {
  body()
} with Attending {
  not_mine(m, resume) -> {
    hear(m)
    resume(())
  }
  return(x) -> x
}
